[
    {
        "content": "<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Vector</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- true</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mf\">1.</span><span class=\"bp\">..</span><span class=\"m\">4</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Application type mismatch:</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- The argument 1...4 has type Std.Rco ?m.11</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm trying to make a Vector of 4 numbers without writing all of them, I assumed that ther range notation ... would allow this, but the type system isn't accepting it. What's the correct approach? Is #v[1...4]  not meant to be used in this sort of scenario?</p>",
        "id": 581054392,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1774257441
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This is <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Vector.range#doc\">docs#Vector.range</a></p>",
        "id": 581057034,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1774258124
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So what is <code>#v[1...4]</code>?</p>",
        "id": 581057095,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1774258141
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not quite, but you can get it with <code>Vector.range'</code>.</p>",
        "id": 581057200,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1774258173
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1027767\">Marvin</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/How.20to.20use.20.23v.5B1.2E.2En.5D.20where.20Vector.20.CE.B1.20n.20is.20expected.3F/near/581057095\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>So what is <code>#v[1...4]</code>?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>As per the error message, it's something of type <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Std.Rco#doc\">docs#Std.Rco</a></p>",
        "id": 581057338,
        "sender_full_name": "Floris van Doorn",
        "timestamp": 1774258205
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well yes but I'm asking whet the difference in purpose is. Those docs say that a Rco is a \"range\", so I'm wondering why this also exists in relation to Vector.range, they both seem to express a vector range.</p>",
        "id": 581057774,
        "sender_full_name": "Marvin",
        "timestamp": 1774258308
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>In the future, you'll be able to write <code>(1...4).toVector</code> just like there is <code>(1...4).toList</code>. The polymorphic ranges are a relatively new feature in the standard library and <code>Vector.range</code> is older. At some point, when the <code>Rco</code> API is sufficiently complete, we'll perhaps deprecate <code>Vector.range</code>, but we're not there yet. Right now, the best you can do if you want to use range notation is <code>(1...4).toArray.toVector</code>, but then the size of the vector will be <code>(1...4).toArray.size</code> and not <code>4 - 1</code>.</p>",
        "id": 581067320,
        "sender_full_name": "Paul Reichert",
        "timestamp": 1774260629
    }
]